{"name":"Honey Bear","hex":"#e8c281","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey bear","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sun bear (Ursus malayanus).","The sloth bear (Melursus ursinus)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey_bear"}],"entries":[{"title":"American black bear","blocks":[{"text":"The American black bear, or simply black bear, is a species of medium-sized bear which is endemic to North America. It is the continent's smallest and most widely distributed bear species. It is an omnivore, with a diet varying greatly depending on season and location. It primarily inhabits forested regions, but may venture beyond forest boundaries in search of food. Individuals are occasionally drawn to human settlements due to the availability of easily accessible food sources."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino_4.jpg/330px-Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_black_bear","wikidata":"Q122783"},{"title":"Sun bear","blocks":[{"text":"The sun bear is a bear species in the family Ursidae found in the tropical forests of Southeast Asia. It is the only species in the genus Helarctos and the smallest bear species, standing nearly 70 cm (28 in) at the shoulder and weighing 25–65 kg (55–143 lb). It is stockily built, with large paws, strongly curved claws, small, rounded ears and a short snout. The fur is generally short and jet black, but can vary from grey to red. The sun bear gets its name from its characteristic orange to cream-coloured chest patch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Sun-bear.jpg/330px-Sun-bear.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_bear","wikidata":"Q144923"},{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"},{"title":"Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae. They are classified as caniforms within the suborder Caniformia. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg/330px-Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear","wikidata":"Q11788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino.jpg/500px-Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino.jpg/1280px-Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino.jpg/500px-Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"U. a. vancouveri turning over rocks for food at low water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVancouver_Island_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus_vancouveri)_Tofino.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Black_bear_Quesnel_Lake_BC.jpg/500px-Black_bear_Quesnel_Lake_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Black_bear_Quesnel_Lake_BC.jpg/500px-Black_bear_Quesnel_Lake_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_bear_Quesnel_Lake_BC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Black_bear_Yellowstone_NP_2008.jpg/500px-Black_bear_Yellowstone_NP_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Black_bear_Yellowstone_NP_2008.jpg/500px-Black_bear_Yellowstone_NP_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_bear_Yellowstone_NP_2008.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Ursus_americanus_PO_03.jpg/500px-Ursus_americanus_PO_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x,